@subject acffa2
@chapter 9
@title Accruals and Prepayments
@lo D4
@topic t1 | The accruals concept | D4
@topic t2 | Accruals and prepayments | D4
@topic t3 | Proforma T-accounts | D4

=== Q001
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: What does the accruals concept require?
+ Income and expenses are recognised in the period they relate to, not when cash moves || Match to the period.
- Only cash transactions are recorded || Cash accounting.
- Expenses are recorded when paid || Ignores timing.
- Income is recorded when received || Ignores timing.
explain: Year-end adjustments make the accounts reflect the period.
mistake: Following cash flows.
tip: Period, not payment.
src: Study text ch9 - accruals concept (concept basis; original question)

=== Q002
type: num
level: F
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
q: A business paid $7,200 for electricity during the year. At the year end, $1,100 is owed for electricity used. What is the electricity expense ($) for the year?
answer: 8300
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Expense = paid + closing accrual
step: Paid = 7,200; owed = 1,100.
step: Expense = 7,200 + 1,100 = $8,300.
check: 7200+1100
explain: The $1,100 is shown as a current liability.
mistake: Charging only the cash paid.
tip: Used but unpaid = add.
src: Study text ch9 - accrued expense (concept basis; original question)

=== Q003
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: How is an ACCRUED expense shown in the statement of financial position?
+ As a current liability || Owed at the year end.
- As a current asset || That is a prepayment.
- As a non-current asset || Not an asset.
- It is not shown || It must be shown.
explain: Entry: Dr expense, Cr accruals.
mistake: Showing accruals as assets.
tip: Owe it = liability.
src: Study text ch9 - presentation (concept basis; original question)

=== Q004
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: How is a PREPAID expense shown in the statement of financial position?
+ As a current asset || Paid for future benefit.
- As a current liability || That is an accrual.
- As revenue || Not income.
- It is not shown || It must be shown.
explain: Entry: Dr prepayments, Cr expense.
mistake: Adding prepayments to the expense.
tip: Paid ahead = asset.
src: Study text ch9 - prepayments (concept basis; original question)

=== Q005
type: num
level: F
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
q: A company received rent of $48,000 during the year, of which $12,000 relates to next year. What rental income ($) is recognised this year?
answer: 36000
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Income = received - amount received in advance
step: Received = 48,000; relating to next year = 12,000.
step: Income = 48,000 - 12,000 = $36,000.
check: 48000-12000
explain: The $12,000 is deferred income, a liability.
mistake: Recognising all cash received.
tip: Received early = liability.
src: Study text ch9 - prepaid income (concept basis; original question)

=== Q006
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: What is the double entry for an accrued expense?
+ Debit expense; credit accruals || Expense up, liability up.
- Debit accruals; credit expense || Reversed.
- Debit prepayments; credit expense || That is a prepayment.
- Debit bank; credit expense || No cash moves.
explain: The accrual reverses in the next period.
mistake: Crediting the expense.
tip: Accrual = Cr accruals.
src: Study text ch9 - entries (concept basis; original question)

=== Q007
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: What is accrued INCOME?
+ Income earned in the period but not yet received || A current asset.
- Income received before it is earned || Deferred income.
- An expense not yet paid || Accrued expense.
- An expense paid in advance || Prepayment.
explain: Entry: Dr accrued income, Cr income.
mistake: Confusing with deferred income.
tip: Earned, not received = asset.
src: Study text ch9 - accrued income (concept basis; original question)

=== Q008
type: num
level: F
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
q: Insurance of $18,000 was paid on 1 October for the 12 months to 30 September next year. The year end is 31 December. What is the prepayment ($) at the year end?
answer: 13500
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Prepayment = payment x months after year end / 12
step: Months relating to next year = January to September = 9.
step: Prepayment = 18,000 x 9/12 = $13,500.
check: 18000*9/12
explain: The expense this year is $4,500 (3 months).
mistake: Using 3 months for the prepayment.
tip: Count months beyond the year end.
src: Study text ch9 - time apportionment (concept basis; original question)

=== Q009
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: What effect does a prepayment have on profit?
+ It reduces the expense, so profit increases || Part of the cost belongs to next year.
- It increases the expense, so profit falls || That is an accrual.
- No effect on profit || It does affect profit.
- It increases revenue || Not revenue.
explain: The prepaid amount is carried forward as an asset.
mistake: Adding prepayments to expenses.
tip: Prepay = smaller expense.
src: Study text ch9 - effect on profit (concept basis; original question)

=== Q010
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: How is income received in advance (deferred income) classified?
+ Current liability || Not yet earned.
- Current asset || That is accrued income.
- Revenue for the current year || Not yet earned.
- Equity || Not equity.
explain: Entry: Dr income, Cr deferred income.
mistake: Treating it as an asset.
tip: Received early = owe the service.
src: Study text ch9 - deferred income (concept basis; original question)

=== Q011
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
scenario: Telephone costs: opening accrual $1,200; cash paid in the year $14,000; closing accrual $1,800.
q: What is the telephone expense ($) for the year?
answer: 14600
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Expense = paid - opening accrual + closing accrual
step: 14,000 - 1,200 = 12,800.
step: 12,800 + 1,800 = $14,600.
check: 14000-1200+1800
explain: Part of this year's payments settled last year's accrual.
mistake: Ignoring the opening accrual.
tip: Opening accrual reduces this year's charge.
src: Study text ch9 - T-account (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q012
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
scenario: Rent: opening prepayment $3,000; cash paid in the year $22,000; closing prepayment $4,000.
q: What is the rent expense ($) for the year?
answer: 21000
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Expense = paid + opening prepayment - closing prepayment
step: 22,000 + 3,000 = 25,000.
step: 25,000 - 4,000 = $21,000.
check: 22000+3000-4000
explain: The opening prepayment is an expense of this year.
mistake: Reversing the prepayment adjustments.
tip: Opening prepayment adds; closing deducts.
src: Study text ch9 - T-account (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q013
type: mcq
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
scenario: Electricity: opening accrual $2,000; opening prepayment $1,500; cash paid $30,000; closing accrual $2,500; closing prepayment $1,000.
q: What is the electricity expense for the year?
+ $31,000 || 30,000 - 2,000 + 2,500 + 1,500 - 1,000.
- $30,000 || Cash only.
- $29,000 || Signs reversed.
- $37,000 || Adds everything.
formula: Expense = paid - opening accrual + closing accrual + opening prepayment - closing prepayment
step: 30,000 - 2,000 + 2,500 = 30,500.
step: 30,500 + 1,500 - 1,000 = $31,000.
check: 30000-2000+2500+1500-1000
explain: Draw the T-account to keep the signs right.
mistake: Reversing one adjustment.
tip: Use the proforma.
src: Study text ch9 - T-account (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q014
type: mr
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
select: 2
q: Which TWO items are current ASSETS?
+ Prepaid insurance || Paid in advance.
+ Interest earned but not yet received || Accrued income.
- Wages owed to staff at the year end || Accrued expense - liability.
- Rent received in advance from a tenant || Deferred income - liability.
explain: Assets: prepayments and accrued income.
mistake: Mixing accrued income and deferred income.
tip: Something owed TO us or paid ahead = asset.
src: Study text ch9 - classification (concept basis; original question)

=== Q015
type: match
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: Match each item with its classification.
pair: Expense incurred but unpaid => Current liability (accrual)
pair: Expense paid in advance => Current asset (prepayment)
pair: Income earned but not received => Current asset (accrued income)
pair: Income received in advance => Current liability (deferred income)
explain: The four adjustments mirror each other.
mistake: Mixing the income items.
tip: Who owes whom?
src: Study text ch9 - classification (concept basis; original question)

=== Q016
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
q: Annual rent was $20,000 until 31 March, when it increased to $24,000 a year. The year end is 31 December. What is the rent expense ($) for the year?
answer: 23000
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Expense = old rate x months / 12 + new rate x months / 12
step: January-March: 20,000 x 3/12 = 5,000.
step: April-December: 24,000 x 9/12 = 18,000; total = $23,000.
check: 20000*3/12+24000*9/12
explain: Time-apportion when rates change.
mistake: Using one rate for the year.
tip: Split by months.
src: Study text ch9 - time apportionment (concept basis; original question)

=== Q017
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
q: A $50,000 loan at 6% a year was taken out on 1 September. No interest has been paid by the year end of 31 December. What interest accrual ($) is needed?
answer: 1000
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Accrual = loan x rate x months / 12
step: Months = September to December = 4.
step: 50,000 x 6% x 4/12 = $1,000.
check: 50000*0.06*4/12
explain: The interest is an expense of this year even though unpaid.
mistake: Charging a full year.
tip: Interest accrues with time.
src: Study text ch9 - interest accrual (concept basis; original question)

=== Q018
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: In the expense T-account, on which side is an OPENING accrual brought forward?
+ Credit side || It was a liability at the start.
- Debit side || That is an opening prepayment.
- Either side || It is fixed.
- It is not brought forward || It is.
explain: An opening prepayment is a debit b/f.
mistake: Debiting opening accruals.
tip: Liability = credit.
src: Study text ch9 - T-accounts (concept basis; original question)

=== Q019
type: mcq
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
q: A company omits a year-end accrual of $900 for cleaning costs. What is the effect?
+ Profit is overstated by $900 and liabilities understated by $900 || Expense missing.
- Profit is understated by $900 || Overstated.
- Assets are overstated || Liabilities understated.
- No effect || Both are wrong.
formula: Expense should include the accrual
step: Cleaning expense is understated by 900.
step: Profit is overstated and the liability is missing.
check: 900
explain: Omitted accruals flatter profit.
mistake: Reversing the effect.
tip: Missing expense = profit too high.
src: Study text ch9 - errors (concept basis; original question)

=== Q020
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: What is the double entry for income received in advance?
+ Debit income; credit deferred income (liability) || Remove it from this year's income.
- Debit deferred income; credit income || Reversed.
- Debit accrued income; credit income || That is accrued income.
- Debit bank; credit income || Already recorded when received.
explain: It will be released to income next year.
mistake: Creating an asset.
tip: Received early = Cr liability.
src: Study text ch9 - deferred income (concept basis; original question)

=== Q021
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
scenario: Rental income: opening accrued income $3,000; opening deferred income $2,000; cash received $40,000; closing accrued income $5,000; closing deferred income $4,000.
q: What rental income ($) is recognised for the year?
answer: 40000
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Income = received - opening accrued + closing accrued + opening deferred - closing deferred
step: 40,000 - 3,000 + 5,000 = 42,000.
step: 42,000 + 2,000 - 4,000 = $40,000.
check: 40000-3000+5000+2000-4000
explain: The adjustments happen to cancel out here.
mistake: Reversing the income adjustments.
tip: Income proforma mirrors expenses.
src: Study text ch9 - income T-account (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q022
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: Where are accruals usually presented in the statement of financial position?
+ Within trade and other payables (current liabilities) || Grouped with payables.
- Within trade and other receivables || That is prepayments.
- Within equity || Not equity.
- Within non-current assets || Not assets.
explain: Prepayments are within trade and other receivables.
mistake: Grouping with receivables.
tip: Accruals with payables.
src: Study text ch9 - presentation (concept basis; original question)

=== Q023
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
q: A year's insurance of $9,600 was paid on 1 May. The year end is 31 December. What is the insurance expense ($) for this year?
answer: 6400
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Expense = payment x months in this year / 12
step: Months in this year = May to December = 8.
step: 9,600 x 8/12 = $6,400 (prepayment $3,200).
check: 9600*8/12
explain: Four months relate to next year.
mistake: Expensing the full amount.
tip: Months used this year.
src: Study text ch9 - prepayments (concept basis; original question)

=== Q024
type: mcq
level: S
cat: trap
topic: t2
status: review
q: A trainee shows a $2,500 accrual for wages owed as a current ASSET. What is wrong?
+ An accrued expense is a liability, so liabilities are understated and assets overstated || Wrong classification.
- Nothing is wrong || It is a liability.
- It should be non-current || It is current.
- It should be in equity || Not equity.
explain: Net assets are overstated by $5,000.
mistake: Calling accruals assets.
tip: Owe staff = liability.
src: Study text ch9 - classification (concept basis; original question)

=== Q025
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: Why are accruals and prepayments needed in addition to recording cash transactions?
+ Cash often moves in a different period from when the expense or income arises || Timing differences.
- To increase profit || Not the purpose.
- To reduce tax || Not the purpose.
- Because cash records are always wrong || Not the reason.
explain: They apply the accruals concept.
mistake: Thinking cash records suffice.
tip: Timing differences.
src: Study text ch9 - purpose (concept basis; original question)

=== Q026
type: order
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: Put the steps of preparing an expense T-account in order.
item: Bring down opening accruals and prepayments
item: Post cash paid during the year
item: Enter closing accruals and prepayments
item: Find the balancing figure as the profit or loss charge
explain: The balancing figure is the expense for the year.
mistake: Forgetting the opening balances.
tip: Open, cash, close, balance.
src: Study text ch9 - T-account (concept basis; original question)

=== Q027
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
q: Commission of $2,400 earned in December will not be received until February. Commission received during the year was $18,600. What commission income ($) is recognised for the year (no opening balances)?
answer: 21000
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Income = received + closing accrued income
step: Received = 18,600; earned but unreceived = 2,400.
step: Income = $21,000.
check: 18600+2400
explain: Accrued income is a current asset.
mistake: Ignoring income earned but unreceived.
tip: Earned counts.
src: Study text ch9 - accrued income (concept basis; original question)

=== Q028
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: An accrual was made at last year end for $800 of gas. What happens to it this year?
+ It reverses: the payment this year clears the liability rather than adding to this year's expense || Opening accrual reduces the charge.
- It is added to this year's expense again || Double counting.
- It is written off to equity || Not correct.
- It becomes a prepayment || Not correct.
explain: The T-account handles this automatically.
mistake: Double counting.
tip: Opening accrual is a credit b/f.
src: Study text ch9 - reversal (concept basis; original question)

=== Q029
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: What is the double entry for a prepaid expense at the year end?
+ Debit prepayments; credit expense || Asset up, expense down.
- Debit expense; credit prepayments || Reversed.
- Debit expense; credit accruals || That is an accrual.
- Debit bank; credit prepayments || No cash moves.
explain: The prepayment is released next year.
mistake: Crediting prepayments.
tip: Prepay = Dr asset.
src: Study text ch9 - entries (concept basis; original question)

=== Q030
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
q: Quarterly rent of $1,500 is payable in arrears. At the year end, one quarter's rent is unpaid. Payments of $4,500 were made in the year. What is the rent expense ($)?
answer: 6000
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Expense = paid + accrual
step: Paid = 4,500 (three quarters); unpaid = 1,500.
step: Expense = $6,000 (four quarters).
check: 4500+1500
explain: A full year's rent must be charged.
mistake: Charging only payments.
tip: Full year's cost.
src: Study text ch9 - accrual (concept basis; original question)

=== Q031
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
scenario: Insurance: at 1 January the prepayment was $2,700. On 1 April the company paid $14,400 for 12 months from 1 April. The year end is 31 December.
q: What is the insurance expense ($) for the year?
answer: 13500
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Expense = opening prepayment + payment - closing prepayment
step: Closing prepayment = 14,400 x 3/12 = 3,600.
step: Expense = 2,700 + 14,400 - 3,600 = $13,500.
check: 2700+14400-14400*3/12
explain: The opening prepayment covered January to March.
mistake: Ignoring the opening prepayment.
tip: Old prepayment + new cost used.
src: Study text ch9 - T-account (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q032
type: mcq
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
scenario: Wages: opening accrual $3,400; paid during the year $96,500; closing accrual $2,900.
q: What wages expense is charged to profit or loss?
+ $96,000 || 96,500 - 3,400 + 2,900.
- $96,500 || Cash only.
- $99,400 || Ignores the opening accrual.
- $102,800 || Adds both.
formula: Expense = paid - opening accrual + closing accrual
step: 96,500 - 3,400 = 93,100.
step: 93,100 + 2,900 = $96,000.
check: 96500-3400+2900
explain: The SOFP shows an accrual of $2,900.
mistake: Adding the opening accrual.
tip: Opening accrual comes off.
src: Study text ch9 - T-account (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q033
type: mr
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
select: 2
q: Which TWO adjustments INCREASE reported profit?
+ Recognising a prepaid expense || Reduces the expense.
+ Recognising accrued income || Increases income.
- Recognising an accrued expense || Increases the expense.
- Recognising deferred income || Reduces income.
explain: Asset adjustments raise profit; liability adjustments lower it.
mistake: Thinking all adjustments lower profit.
tip: Asset side = profit up.
src: Study text ch9 - effects (concept basis; original question)

=== Q034
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
scenario: A tenant pays rent of $3,000 per quarter in advance on 1 February, 1 May, 1 August and 1 November. The landlord's year end is 31 December.
q: How much deferred income ($) does the landlord show at 31 December?
answer: 1000
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Deferred = advance payment x months after year end / 3
step: The 1 November payment covers November to January.
step: January relates to next year: 3,000 x 1/3 = $1,000.
check: 3000/3
explain: Rental income for the year is 12,000 - 1,000 + any opening deferral.
mistake: Deferring the whole quarter.
tip: Months into next year.
src: Study text ch9 - deferred income (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q035
type: mcq
level: C
cat: trap
topic: t3
status: review
q: A trainee calculates an expense as "cash paid + opening prepayment + closing prepayment". What is wrong?
+ The closing prepayment should be DEDUCTED, as it relates to next year || Wrong sign.
- Nothing is wrong || Sign error.
- The opening prepayment should be deducted || It is added.
- Prepayments should be ignored || They must be included.
explain: Expense = paid + opening prepayment - closing prepayment.
mistake: Adding the closing prepayment.
tip: Closing prepayment = next year.
src: Study text ch9 - T-account (concept basis; original question)

=== Q036
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
scenario: A $120,000 loan at 5% a year was taken out on 1 March. Interest is paid half-yearly in arrears; the first payment of $3,000 was made on 31 August. The year end is 31 December.
q: What interest accrual ($) is required at the year end?
answer: 2000
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Accrual = interest for months unpaid
step: Interest for September to December = 120,000 x 5% x 4/12 = 2,000.
step: Accrual = $2,000 (total expense 5,000).
check: 120000*0.05*4/12
explain: Interest from 1 September has not yet been paid.
mistake: Accruing a full half-year.
tip: Unpaid months only.
src: Study text ch9 - interest (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q037
type: mcq
level: C
cat: scenario
topic: t1
status: review
q: A company pays a 2-year software maintenance contract of $24,000 in advance at the start of Year 1. How should this be treated at the end of Year 1?
+ Expense $12,000; prepayment of $12,000 as a current asset || Next year's service is prepaid.
- Expense $24,000 in Year 1 || Ignores accruals.
- Capitalise $24,000 as a non-current asset || A service, not an asset to depreciate here.
- Expense nothing until Year 2 || Year 1 benefited.
explain: Only the portion relating to Year 1 is an expense.
mistake: Expensing all cash paid.
tip: Spread over the service period.
src: Study text ch9 - prepayments (concept basis; original scenario)

=== Q038
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
scenario: Rates: opening prepayment $1,800; cash paid $9,600 on 1 April for the 12 months to 31 March next year. Year end 31 December.
q: What is the rates expense ($) for the year?
answer: 9000
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Expense = opening prepayment + paid - closing prepayment
step: Closing prepayment = 9,600 x 3/12 = 2,400.
step: Expense = 1,800 + 9,600 - 2,400 = $9,000.
check: 1800+9600-9600*3/12
explain: January to March was covered by last year's payment.
mistake: Ignoring the opening balance.
tip: Use the T-account.
src: Study text ch9 - T-account (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q039
type: mcq
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: A company records a prepayment of $3,000 when it should have recorded an accrual of $3,000 for the same expense. What is the effect on profit?
+ Profit is overstated by $6,000 || The expense is $3,000 too low instead of $3,000 too high.
- Profit is overstated by $3,000 || The swing is double.
- No effect || There is an effect.
- Profit is understated by $6,000 || Overstated.
formula: Swing = prepayment wrongly deducted + accrual wrongly omitted
step: The prepayment reduces the expense by 3,000.
step: The missing accrual leaves it 3,000 too low as well: 6,000 in total.
check: 3000+3000
explain: Net assets are also overstated by $6,000.
mistake: Thinking the error is $3,000.
tip: Wrong direction = double effect.
src: Study text ch9 - errors (concept basis; original question)

=== Q040
type: match
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: Match each year-end adjustment with its double entry.
pair: Accrued expense => Dr expense, Cr accruals
pair: Prepaid expense => Dr prepayments, Cr expense
pair: Accrued income => Dr accrued income, Cr income
pair: Deferred income => Dr income, Cr deferred income
explain: Each adjustment moves an amount into the correct period.
mistake: Reversing entries.
tip: Asset debit, liability credit.
src: Study text ch9 - entries (concept basis; original question)

=== Q041
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
scenario: Interest receivable account: opening accrued income $600; cash received $4,200; closing accrued income $900.
q: What interest income ($) is recognised for the year?
answer: 4500
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Income = received - opening accrued income + closing accrued income
step: 4,200 - 600 = 3,600.
step: 3,600 + 900 = $4,500.
check: 4200-600+900
explain: Part of the cash received was last year's income.
mistake: Adding the opening accrued income.
tip: Opening accrued income comes off.
src: Study text ch9 - income T-account (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q042
type: mcq
level: C
cat: scenario
topic: t2
status: review
q: A gym sells annual memberships for cash in advance. At the year end, many memberships still have months to run. How should the unused portion be treated?
+ As deferred income (a current liability) until the service is provided || Not yet earned.
- As revenue when cash is received || Ignores accruals.
- As accrued income || That is earned but unreceived.
- As a prepayment asset || The gym received the cash.
explain: Revenue is recognised as the service is provided.
mistake: Recognising all cash as revenue.
tip: Owe the service = liability.
src: Study text ch9 - deferred income (concept basis; original scenario)

=== Q043
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
scenario: Light and heat: opening accrual $650; closing accrual $720; expense charged in profit or loss $8,400.
q: How much cash ($) was paid for light and heat during the year?
answer: 8330
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Paid = expense + opening accrual - closing accrual
step: 8,400 + 650 = 9,050.
step: 9,050 - 720 = $8,330.
check: 8400+650-720
explain: Work back through the T-account.
mistake: Reversing the accruals.
tip: Rearrange the proforma.
src: Study text ch9 - working back (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q044
type: mcq
level: C
cat: trap
topic: t2
status: review
q: A trainee classifies rent received in advance from a tenant as "accrued income". What is wrong?
+ It is deferred income, a liability, not accrued income, an asset || Received before earned.
- Nothing is wrong || It is deferred income.
- It should be a prepaid expense || Not an expense.
- It should be ignored || It must be recorded.
explain: Accrued income is earned but not yet received.
mistake: Mixing up the income terms.
tip: Cash first = deferred.
src: Study text ch9 - classification (concept basis; original question)

=== Q045
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
scenario: A business pays a cleaning contractor $450 per month in arrears; the December invoice is paid in January. Payments made in the year (January to December) totalled $5,400, including last year's December invoice.
q: What is the cleaning expense ($) for the year?
answer: 5400
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Expense = paid - opening accrual + closing accrual
step: Opening accrual (last December) = 450; closing accrual (this December) = 450.
step: Expense = 5,400 - 450 + 450 = $5,400 (12 months x 450).
check: 5400-450+450
explain: When the accruals are equal, the expense equals cash paid.
mistake: Adding the closing accrual without removing the opening one.
tip: Twelve months' cost.
src: Study text ch9 - accruals (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q046
type: mcq
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: Which Conceptual Framework idea underlies accruals and prepayments?
+ Accrual accounting - effects are shown in the periods in which they occur || Not cash timing.
- Going concern || Related, but not the core idea here.
- Business entity || Not relevant.
- Materiality || Not the core idea.
explain: Accrual accounting gives a better picture of performance than cash.
mistake: Citing going concern.
tip: Accruals basis.
src: Study text ch9 - framework (concept basis; original question)

=== Q047
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
scenario: Motor expenses: opening prepayment (insurance) $1,100; opening accrual (fuel) $380; cash paid $12,600; closing prepayment $1,250; closing accrual $420.
q: What is the motor expense charge ($) for the year?
answer: 12490
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Expense = paid + opening prepayment - opening accrual - closing prepayment + closing accrual
step: 12,600 + 1,100 - 380 = 13,320.
step: 13,320 - 1,250 + 420 = $12,490.
check: 12600+1100-380-1250+420
explain: One account can hold both an accrual and a prepayment for different items.
mistake: Netting the opening balances on the wrong sides.
tip: Prepayments debit; accruals credit.
src: Study text ch9 - combined T-account (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q048
type: mcq
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: Why might a business choose not to accrue a very small expense, such as a $15 invoice received after the year end?
+ Materiality - the amount would not affect users' decisions || Cost of adjusting outweighs benefit.
- Because accruals are optional || They are required where material.
- Because small invoices are illegal || Not true.
- To increase profit deliberately || Not a valid reason.
explain: Consistency in applying a threshold is important.
mistake: Thinking every cent must be accrued.
tip: Immaterial = may ignore.
src: Study text ch9 - materiality (concept basis; original scenario)

=== Q049
type: mcq
level: F
cat: revision
topic: t2
status: review
important: 1
q: Which statement about year-end adjustments is correct?
+ Accrued expenses and deferred income are liabilities; prepayments and accrued income are assets || Core classification.
- All four adjustments are liabilities || Two are assets.
- All four adjustments are assets || Two are liabilities.
- Prepayments are liabilities || They are assets.
explain: Each adjustment moves income or expense into the right period.
mistake: Mixing classifications.
tip: Owe = liability; owed or paid ahead = asset.
src: Study text ch9 - summary (concept basis; original question)

=== Q050
type: mcq
level: S
cat: revision
topic: t3
status: review
important: 1
q: Which formula gives the profit or loss expense charge?
+ Cash paid - opening accrual + closing accrual + opening prepayment - closing prepayment || Proforma result.
- Cash paid + opening accrual - closing accrual || Signs reversed.
- Cash paid only || Ignores accruals.
- Cash paid + all accruals and prepayments || Wrong signs.
explain: The income proforma mirrors this with accrued and deferred income.
mistake: Reversing signs.
tip: Draw the T-account.
src: Study text ch9 - proforma (concept basis; original question)
